---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import DeploymentTopology from "../components/diagrams/DeploymentTopology.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Deployment | BeaconGuard Controlled Deployment Qualification"
  description="Review BeaconGuard’s controlled deployment qualification model for regulated enterprise environments, including identity, recovery, evidence, and cross-platform operating paths."
>
  <section class="stack-section">
    <p class="section-label">DEPLOYMENT POSTURE</p>
    <h1>Controlled deployment qualification for regulated environments</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      BeaconGuard deploys alongside existing enterprise applications and places a deterministic Runtime Control Boundary before governed AI, APIs, or automated systems execute. The platform is currently in controlled internal qualification across installation, operation, recovery, rollback, and cross-platform packaging.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Deployment without system replacement</h2>
    <p>
      BeaconGuard deploys alongside existing applications. Existing systems continue to own workflow logic, systems of record, identity, case management, logging, and compliance processes. BeaconGuard places a Runtime Control Boundary before governed AI, APIs, or automated systems execute, returns ALLOW or DENY, and emits Verifiable Decision Records.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Qualified operational paths</h2>
    <p>
      The following operational paths are evaluated under controlled internal qualification. Public package downloads and general installer distribution are not implied.
    </p>
    <div class="section-grid section-grid--scenarios">
      <div class="content-card">
        <h3>Clean installation and initialization</h3>
        <p>Controlled setup and readiness for a named environment under shared deployment ownership.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="content-card">
        <h3>Runtime operation</h3>
        <p>Deterministic evaluation, ALLOW or DENY enforcement, and Verifiable Decision Record emission during governed requests.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="content-card">
        <h3>Backup, restore, and rollback</h3>
        <p>Recovery and rollback expectations reviewed against the named environment and shared responsibilities.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="content-card">
        <h3>Removal and reinstall</h3>
        <p>Controlled removal and reinstall paths for qualification and operational continuity planning.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="content-card">
        <h3>Restart continuity</h3>
        <p>Restart and continuity behavior for the control and evidence boundary under fail-closed assumptions.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="content-card">
        <h3>Windows and Linux paths</h3>
        <p>Windows and Linux packaging paths evaluated in controlled environments, including container and OCI-oriented deployment compatibility where supported by the deployment documentation.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Deployment qualification model</h2>
    <div class="section-grid section-grid--scenarios">
      <div class="content-card">
        <h3>1. Architecture and workflow review</h3>
        <p>Confirm boundary placement, request context, Compliance Playbook scope, identity dependencies, evidence requirements, and operating responsibilities.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="content-card">
        <h3>2. Target-environment qualification</h3>
        <p>Evaluate host, network, identity, storage, evidence, recovery, and application-control assumptions for the named environment.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="content-card">
        <h3>3. Controlled deployment planning</h3>
        <p>Define installation, release verification, operational ownership, rollback, monitoring, and acceptance criteria before broader use.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Placement and intent</h2>
    <p>
      BeaconGuard belongs on the runtime path between existing application services and the governed AI, API, or automated system. An optional System Connector can map source protocols into the required request format. The connector does not evaluate controls or activate releases. Evidence persists before ALLOW proceeds; DENY hard-stops with no downstream execution. The Control Plane operates outside the per-request path.
    </p>
    <DeploymentTopology />
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Trust boundaries and responsibilities</h2>
    <p>
      Only an Active Verified Release governs evaluation at the Runtime Control Boundary. Applications must enforce ALLOW and DENY outcomes and must not silently bypass the boundary. When required release material, signed metadata, trust signals, approved-pathway status, approvals, freshness, or replay inputs are missing or invalid, decisions fail closed as DENY. Human review via a Verifiable Decision Record obligation does not permit execution.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Customer IAM and identity authority</li>
      <li>Network path controls</li>
      <li>Host hardening</li>
      <li>Secret custody</li>
      <li>Release-signing custody</li>
      <li>Environment separation</li>
      <li>Evidence-provider configuration</li>
      <li>Monitoring and incident response</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Availability</h2>
    <p>
      BeaconGuard is currently in controlled internal qualification. Deployment scope, environment assumptions, and evaluation access are reviewed through Controlled Evaluation.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/docs/deployment-integration">Deployment and integration</a></li>
      <li><a href="/docs/compliance-audit">Compliance and audit</a></li>
      <li><a href="/how-it-works">How BeaconGuard enforces decisions</a></li>
    </ul>
    <div class="page-actions">
      <a class="btn-primary" href="/design-partner-program">Request Controlled Evaluation</a>
      <a class="btn-secondary" href="/trust-assurance">Understand Trust Center boundaries</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
